{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794583243,"nonce":"TmF+Htk5cTtPTLYde4XIz9BRm3Q=","message":"Tuple values now support pair[index] reads and writes through an abstract over Array\u003cDynamic\u003e, as requested. The public type remains hxn.Tuple\u003cA, B, ...\u003e, with Tuple.of, explicitly typed constructors, numbered record inputs, fixed declared length, copying, and independent inspection arrays. Typed _0, _1, etc. properties remain for compatibility and for the generated serializers; the runtime value is an array, so nested tuple[0][1] access also works.\n\nHaxe 4.3.6 rejects a macro @:arrayAccess method with \"Invalid modifier: macro on array-access function\". A heterogeneous tuple therefore cannot select a static return type from the numeric [] position. [] returns and accepts Dynamic and checks bounds at runtime. pair.at(1) and pair.set(1, value) are ordinary member macros that keep the position's static type; they require an integer literal and reject out-of-bounds positions or wrongly typed assignments during compilation. Dynamic indexing retains normal Haxe Dynamic semantics, including the need for static typing when a method must be retained by DCE. The bytes fixture calls at(1).toString for that reason.\n\nA generic-build macro defines one typed position abstract per arity. Its getters and setters map positions onto the array, and record conversion copies each position into a new array. A common outer abstract provides indexing, copy/toArray, and typed member macros. Both layers erase to the same array, so no wrapper object or per-arity runtime class is needed, and CPPIA uses the host's ordinary array representation. Rust, the wire encoding, and manifest names are unchanged. No runtime support crate or native ABI changed.\n\nThe counter fixture uses [] throughout its tuple checks, including nested tuples, returned values, collections, futures, signals, streams, callbacks, and wide integers. Additional checks cover runtime index evaluation, compound-assignment receiver evaluation, typed accessor receiver evaluation, reads/writes outside the bounds, and preservation of the tuple after a refused index. The packaged tuple probe exercises [] and typed access; nine invalid programs cover wrong types and arity, empty tuples, typed access with the wrong expected type, wrong setter type, invalid literal bounds, and a nonliteral typed position. The pantry example prints name/quantity pairs through pair[0] and pair[1]. Design and convention documents describe both access forms.\n\nValidation, with runtime tests under timeouts:\n- macOS arm64: workspace tests, strict Clippy, formatting, and shell syntax checks pass. All nine counter modes pass. CLI and examples pass on Ash hybrid/JIT/interpreter, hxcpp, CPPIA, Ash wasm and Emscripten; C imports pass in every supported mode, with native SQLite where supported. Generated Ash hybrid/JIT/interpreter, hxcpp and CPPIA pass three repetitions each, and generated wasm/Emscripten pass twice after their initial runs.\n- Linux x86-64 through ssh nuc: workspace tests, strict Clippy, stock HashLink fixtures, generated Ash/hxcpp/CPPIA fixtures, and all native CLI/example/import modes pass. The generated program passes three repetitions each on stock HashLink and Ash hybrid/JIT/interpreter. SHA-256 verification includes every tracked local file and the two new tuple support files. Haxe/hxcpp configuration remains task-local.\n- Mobile: the full example suite passes twice each on the iPhone 15 iOS 17.5 simulator through iphonesim and the Pixel 9 Pro Android emulator through ADB. Both devices were shut down afterwards.\n\nNot run: Windows (awaiting a push and CI), Linux wasm/Emscripten (both paths ran on macOS), or a physical mobile device. The optional NUC SQLite import is skipped because sqlite3.h is absent. Miri was not rerun because hxn-abi and hxn-future did not change.\n\nThe tuple wrapper and indexing changes remain uncommitted. The broader generator issue stays open for its remaining gaps.","files":null}]}